      <SupplementalInformationDetail>
        <FormAndLineReferenceDesc>Part II, Line 5: Summarized Policy</FormAndLineReferenceDesc>
        <ExplanationTxt>Monitoring and inspection is outlined in a project-specific Habitat Management Plan (HMP) or similar document (Preserve Management Plans, Open Space Management Plans, etc). A HMP presents required management and monitoring tasks as approved by the resource agencies (U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, California Department of Fish and Game, Army Corps of Engineers) who have issued permits for a project. The HMP compiles the various permit requirements into one document. In most cases, a conservation easement is placed on the preserved land that limits any activity on the land except for habitat preservation and management of that open space. Depending on the violation, enforcement of the easement is coordinated through the owner or managing entity, local law enforcement, the municipality, and the resource agencies mentioned above.</ExplanationTxt>
      </SupplementalInformationDetail>
      <SupplementalInformationDetail>
        <FormAndLineReferenceDesc>Part II, Line 9: Organization Reporting of conservation easements.</FormAndLineReferenceDesc>
        <ExplanationTxt>SDHC RECORDS REVENUE WHEN THE CONSERVATION EASEMENT AGREEMENTS HAVE BEEN SIGNED AND FUNDING FOR THE EASEMENTS HAS BEEN DETERMINED. EXPENSES ARE RECORDED WHEN WORK ON THE CONSERVATION EASEMENT HAS BEEN INCURRED.</ExplanationTxt>
      </SupplementalInformationDetail>
      <SupplementalInformationDetail>
        <FormAndLineReferenceDesc>Part V, Line 4: Intended uses of the endowment fund.</FormAndLineReferenceDesc>
        <ExplanationTxt>THE ENDOWMENT FUNDS CONSIST OF SEVERAL INDIVIDUAL FUNDS ESTABLISHED FOR A VARIETY OF PURPOSES RELATED TO THE ENTITY'S EXEMPT PURPOSE.</ExplanationTxt>
      </SupplementalInformationDetail>
      <SupplementalInformationDetail>
        <FormAndLineReferenceDesc>Part X : FIN48 Footnote</FormAndLineReferenceDesc>
        <ExplanationTxt>The Conservancy is a public charity and is exempt from income taxes under Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code and Section 23701(d) of the California Revenue and Taxation Code. The Conservancy believes that it has appropriate support for any tax positions taken, and as such, does not have any uncertain tax positions that are material to the financial statements. The Conservancy is not a private foundation.The Conservancys Return of Organization Exempt from Income Tax for the years ended December 31, 2024, 2023, 2022, and 2021 are subject to examination by the Internal Revenue Service and State taxing authorities, generally three to four years after the returns were filed.</ExplanationTxt>
      </SupplementalInformationDetail>
